<Base
  lang="en"
  page="overview"
  eyebrow="A home system, built for a hackathon"
  title="An activity on paper, invented for one person"
  description="Lanternina invents an activity for an adolescent, has a parent approve it, and runs it on paper and e-paper. Models in Microsoft Foundry; nothing infers on the device."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Lanternina invents an activity for an adolescent, has a parent approve it, and then runs it in
    a real room — on paper, on e-paper displays, and on physical buttons. It reads what comes back
    in handwriting, decides what happens next from that, and stops at an hour the parent set.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mini-PC in a house drives two e-paper displays, an inkjet and a flatbed scanner. Every
    language and vision call goes to Microsoft Foundry in the EU. No model runs on the device, and
    there is no offline mode. The house holds the clock and the sealing keys, and it is the only
    thing that starts work.
  </p>

  <figure class="band">
    <img
      src="/images/hero.jpg"
      width="1800"
      height="771"
      alt="A 7.5-inch e-paper display in a 3D printed frame, showing an engraving."
    />
    <figcaption>
      One of two displays. The frame is 3D printed and the unit was assembled by hand; the picture
      on it was generated an hour earlier from a theme the parent wrote.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>How an activity goes</h2>

  <p>
    Seven steps, in the order they happen. The house starts all of them; nothing in the cloud can
    start anything.
  </p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <b>The house asks whether there is an activity to be had.</b> The mini-PC asks on its own
      clock, and the answer arrives inside the reply. Approving something in the dashboard writes a
      row and does nothing else, and the house finds that row the next time it asks. No path leads
      into a device in a room, which is also why there is nothing on the home network to attack.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>A model writes the whole activity in one answer.</b> Every moment of it, three lengths for
      each moment, a four-rung ladder of help under each, and a way out from anywhere. Nothing is
      added while it runs. That is what lets the same moment be shortened when the clock is tight,
      or put on a display that holds forty words, and still reach the same ending.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Seven checks and a safety gate read it before anybody does.</b> The checks read its shape:
      that every moment can be reached, that the way out reaches for something already in the
      person's hands, that no question is asked the activity never answers. A refused activity is
      handed back to be repaired, once. The gate reads the words.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>A parent reads the overview and approves it.</b> Once, and from the overview rather than
      moment by moment: approving every branch would need an adult awake at the moment each branch
      was taken. The parent also sets the hour the activity has to be over by.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The house prints a page.</b> An image model is given every string that has already passed
      the gate, told to letter exactly those characters, and draws the whole A4 sheet in one pass —
      a map, a museum label, a leaf out of a field notebook. The page is described in words rather
      than laid out in code, so the design work is the prompt, and the format lives in one Markdown
      file.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Somebody writes on it and puts it back on the glass.</b> The paper carries only what a
      person reads: a model is handed two images, the blank as it was printed and what came back,
      and asked what is different. An earlier version printed a QR code, corner markers and a grid
      of declared boxes, and all three were deleted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>What came back decides what happens next, and then it ends.</b> The rest of the activity
      is written while the sheet is still on the table. Thirty minutes before the hour the parent
      set, whatever has been reached, the way out begins — and it arrives at the same ending, never
      announced as a shortened one.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What comes out, and what goes back</h2>

  <p>
    The page below was drawn whole by an image model, from words that had already been screened.
    Somebody writes on it and puts it back on the scanner glass; the reading decides the next
    moments, which are written while the sheet is still on the table.
  </p>

  <div class="pair">
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/page.jpg"
        width="919"
        height="1300"
        loading="lazy"
        alt="A printed page, blank."
      />
      <figcaption>Handed over. Unedited model output, 19–33 s to draw.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/page-written.jpg"
        width="866"
        height="1300"
        loading="lazy"
        alt="The same page, filled in by hand."
      />
      <figcaption>
        Came back. Filled in here by a simulated hand, so a whole run can be exercised with no
        person in it.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    The reader's vocabulary is deliberately poor: a page came back with <b>marks</b> or
    <b>blank</b>, and nothing else. Not a count and not a fraction, because a count of somebody's
    marks is one step from a score. The reading is then gone — the type refuses to be pickled,
    copied or cached.
  </p>

  <h2>Measured on the deployment</h2>

  <p>
    Dates are attached because prompts change and the numbers move with them. Everything here was
    measured against the real services, not estimated.
  </p>

  <table class="numbers">
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Devising a whole activity</th>
        <td>68–157 s</td>
        <td class="when">n=22, Sep 2026</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Rereading it with a second model</th>
        <td>14.4–22.8 s</td>
        <td class="when">Sep 2026</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Drawing one page</th>
        <td>19–33 s</td>
        <td class="when">Aug 2026</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Ink on a drawn page</th>
        <td>0.5–2.7 %</td>
        <td class="when">Aug 2026</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Reading a returned page</th>
        <td>4.4–5.5 s</td>
        <td class="when">Aug 2026</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Button press to reading on the display</th>
        <td>≈ 35 s</td>
        <td class="when">19 Aug 2026</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Printed ruler, measured with a ruler</th>
        <td>50 mm asked, 50 mm got</td>
        <td class="when">4 Aug 2026</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="note">
    The last row is the one that is not about speed. The print chain does not rescale, and that was
    checked by hand — a chain that silently rescales would make every physical dimension on every
    page wrong in a way no test would notice.
  </p>

  <h2>The stack</h2>

  <h3>In the house</h3>
  <p>
    Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5 on a PoE base board, Raspberry Pi OS. Two 7.5" e-paper displays on
    XIAO ESP32-S3, running a patched TRMNL firmware against our own BYOS server. Epson ET-2870 over
    IPP and eSCL, driverless. Frames 3D printed, units assembled by hand.
  </p>

  <h3>In the cloud</h3>
  <p>
    Microsoft Foundry in the EU for every language, vision and image call, behind a single router
    module that is the only place allowed to import a model SDK. Azure AI Content Safety on
    everything generated and on every inbound image. Container Apps for the API, Cosmos DB, Blob
    Storage, Static Web Apps. Infrastructure as Bicep.
  </p>

  <h3>How it ships</h3>
  <p>
    A push to <code>main</code> publishes the API through a user-assigned managed identity with a
    federated credential, so there is no password anywhere in the pipeline — measured end to end at
    1 m 35 s. The image is built on the runner rather than with <code>az acr build</code>, because
    the latter needs rights that would let the identity reconfigure the registry.
  </p>

  <figure class="narrow">
    <img
      src="/images/kit.jpg"
      width="825"
      height="1100"
      loading="lazy"
      alt="An e-paper panel, an ESP32 board and a battery, before assembly."
    />
    <figcaption>
      What goes into a frame: panel, XIAO ESP32-S3, a 2000 mAh cell, and the ribbon between them.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What it does not do</h2>

  <p>
    Engagement optimisation is easy to add to a system like this and would do harm, so it is a
    written rule rather than a matter of judgement. <code>tests/test_boundaries.py</code> refuses the
    vocabulary in the stored shape, in the panel, in the browser and in the prompts. Each of those
    tests was checked by injecting the violation and watching the test fail.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>No streak, no daily goal, nothing withheld as a reason to come back.</li>
    <li>No notification triggered by absence, and no measure of time spent.</li>
    <li>
      A profile is kept, and it reaches the two models that write and run an activity — and no
      display, no printed sheet and no page of the panel. A review gate enforces that, not the hope
      that a prompt was polite.
    </li>
    <li>No face or person detection, no emotion or attention inference, no biometrics.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    An agent also has nowhere to record that its own output is acceptable: <code>Proposal</code> has
    no status field, and only the safety gate can mint the type a user-facing payload requires. The
    boundary is a type rather than a convention, so breaking it does not compile.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>Why each boundary exists, and what it costs</a
